    "created_at": "2016-08-26T16:48:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-26T16:48:30Z",
    "body": "> The Chrome implementation does not make it seem like the restriction for legacy values is restricted to `<meta name=referrer>`\n\nThat's true. @estark37, any plans to change Chrome to use more strict parsing for the other call sites, e.g. [for the header](https://cs.chromium.org/chromium/src/third_party/WebKit/Source/core/loader/FrameLoader.cpp?sq=package:chromium&rcl=1472210497&l=533)?\n\n> Also, does the split operation in Chrome do anything with whitespace? Seems unlikely whitespace would not be trimmed.\n\nIn Chrome it does not. I guess that is another spec/Chrome mismatch.\n\n> Given that Firefox does not implement the more complicated parsing, should we really align with Chrome here?\n\nI would love @estark37's thoughts here. I think there's value in allowing fallback values as explained in https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-referrer-policy/#unknown-policy-values, for both the header and the meta tag. But I agree it seems kind of messy (e.g. the disallowing whitespace).\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
