    "created_at": "2016-08-26T16:55:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-26T16:55:17Z",
    "body": "> That's true. @estark37, any plans to change Chrome to use more strict parsing for the other call sites, e.g. for the header?\n\nYeah, that's in the commit queue as we speak: https://codereview.chromium.org/2278823004/\n\n> I think there's value in allowing fallback values as explained in https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-referrer-policy/#unknown-policy-values, for both the header and the meta tag. But I agree it seems kind of messy (e.g. the disallowing whitespace).\n\nThe fallback values are valuable, I think, but could an author include multiple meta referrer elements to get that behavior? That is, maybe each meta referrer need only contain a single token, and you can have multiple meta referrer elements to get the fallback behavior?\n",
