    "created_at": "2016-08-26T17:27:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-26T17:27:08Z",
    "body": "> Good point! Yeah, the later ones in the document will override the earlier ones, if they contain valid referrer policies.\n\nRight now the spec says to abort the algorithm after encountering the first valid policy (step 2.2.2 of https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#meta-referrer); I think we just need to delete \"and abort this algorithm\".\n\n> Should we specific that Firefox behavior of only matching a single token, and are you willing to implement it in Chrome? It would be backward-incompatible, because things like no-referrer-when-downgrade,unsafe-url would be just ignored instead of picking the last one :-/.\n\nI suspect there is very low usage of the comma-separated syntax. I might be misremembering but I think it's possible that we didn't support that until we implemented the Referrer-Policy header, which was pretty recently. I'd be on-board with specifying the FF single-token behavior, and then I can add a UseCounter in hopes that we can remove support for the comma-separated syntax in Chrome. How does that sound?\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
